  "query_preview": "..\nThursday: Broad ridge pattern will keep dry, warm conditions \nover the region for the next several days. Winds will be light, \ndiurnal driven with strongest winds along the east side of the \nCascades. Wind gusts will be in the teens for the area. For \ntemperatures, highs will climb into the 90s with humidity in the\nteens and brief periods single digits across the Basin. \nWildfire smoke will play a big part on the highs. It has been\ncausing the previous few days highs to run cooler than the\nmodels. Overnight lows will be in the 50s to low 60s.\n\nFriday and Saturday: The ridge axis shifts east and weakens and\nbrings a zonal flow pattern. Winds will increase as warm dry \nconditions continue. Winds will be West with sustained speeds in\nthe teens and gusts into the 20 to 30 mph range. The strongest \nwinds are most likely to remain confined to portions of central \nWashington including the east Cascade valleys and the Waterville\nPlateau. There is around a 40-70% chance of wind gusts up to \n30-35 mph in those locations in central WA Friday and \nSaturday. One major difference from August 1 is the lack of \nstrong low level winds. 850-700mb winds are only reaching into \nthe 10-20 mph range compared to the 30-40 mph from August 1. It \nis still an elevated to critical fire weather period due to the \ncontinued hot and dry conditions with the breezy winds. Fire \nWeather Watches have been issued for breezy westerly winds and \nlow relative humidity both Friday and Saturday. Highs will be in\nthe upper 80s and 90s with humidity in the teens to low 20s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s to low 60s. Minimum relative \nhumidity will reach the 10-25% range with limited recovery \novernight.\n\nSunday through Monday: Cooler air mass will filter into the \nregion. Winds will remain diurnally driven with the potential \nfor some locally breezy winds through the Cascade gaps.There is \nstill no decent chance of of precip expected on for the period. \nHighs will dip into the 80s. Lows will be in the upper 40s and \n50s. /JDC",
